Spaceships — running locally
============================

Prerequisites
-------------
- Node.js 18+ (check with `node --version`). The server uses ES modules and
  `node --watch`, both available since Node 18.
- npm comes bundled with Node.

First-time setup
----------------
From the project root (the folder that has package.json):

    npm install

This downloads Express and the websocket dependency into node_modules/.
You only need to run this once, or again if dependencies change.

Run the server
--------------
    npm start

You should see:

    Spaceships server listening on port 4000
    Local:  http://localhost:4000
    LAN:    http://<your-ip>:4000

Open http://localhost:4000 in a browser. The lobby loads and you can play.

For development with auto-reload on server file changes:

    npm run dev

(restarts the server when files under server/ change; the browser still
needs a manual refresh)

Play with friends on your LAN
-----------------------------
Find your machine's local IP:

    macOS:    ipconfig getifaddr en0
    Linux:    hostname -I
    Windows:  ipconfig    (look for IPv4 Address)

Share that IP with someone on the same Wi-Fi:

    http://192.168.x.x:4000

Both browsers connect to the same server, then one player creates a room
and the other joins with the 4-letter room code.

Different port
--------------
The server reads PORT from the environment:

    PORT=3000 npm start

Useful if 4000 is busy.

Troubleshooting
---------------
- "address already in use" — something else is on port 4000. Pick a different
  port with `PORT=3001 npm start`, or find and kill the offender:
      lsof -ti:4000 | xargs kill
- Page loads but lobby shows "Open via http://localhost:4000" — you opened
  index.html directly via file://. Always go through the server URL.
- WebSocket fails on the LAN — check firewall settings. macOS may prompt to
  allow Node to accept incoming connections the first time.
